From: Gauthier Piarrette <gauthierpiarrette@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] winecoreaudio: Keep a render stream's I/O buffer under half a period.

Adapted for CrossOver 26.3 from gauthierpiarrette/highball-engine
commit c9b8b9735643603e9b10d8fbee069cc35ca552cd (patch 0015); only the
comment is reworded. A downstream fix, not a WineHQ backport.

The HAL output unit asks the render callback for its whole I/O buffer
at once, 512 frames by default, which at 48 kHz is 10.7 ms: longer
than the 10 ms period a shared-mode client is woken for, and longer
than the Windows audio engine's own processing period. A client that
keeps about one period queued then has too little at most pulls, and
the rest of each pull is played as silence. Measured on an M4 at
48 kHz: Counter-Strike 2's menu music dropped out 324 times in two
minutes, and not at all at 96 kHz, where the same 512 frames last
5.3 ms, or with this change.

Before initializing a render stream's unit, lower its I/O buffer to
half the stream period at the device rate when it is larger. The
property is per process, so other applications keep theirs; failure
to read or set it leaves the buffer as it was.

diff --git a/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c b/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
index c2bb765..ae46806 100644
--- a/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
+++ b/dlls/winecoreaudio.drv/coreaudio.c
@@ -800,6 +800,30 @@ static NTSTATUS unix_create_stream(void *args)
                            "This can be enabled under Security & Privacy in System Preferences.\n");
     }
 
+    if(stream->flow == eRender){
+        /* Keep each pull from the device under half a period. The device asks for its
+         * whole I/O buffer at once, 512 frames by default, which at 48 kHz is 10.7 ms,
+         * longer than the 10 ms period a shared-mode client is woken for, so a client
+         * keeping about one period queued had too little at most pulls and the rest was
+         * played as silence. The buffer size is per process, so other apps keep theirs. */
+        AudioStreamBasicDescription hw;
+        UInt32 hw_size = sizeof(hw), cur = 0, cur_size = sizeof(cur), frames;
+        if(AudioUnitGetProperty(stream->unit, kAudioUnitProperty_StreamFormat, kAudioUnitScope_Output, 0,
+                                &hw, &hw_size) == noErr && hw.mSampleRate > 0 &&
+           AudioUnitGetProperty(stream->unit, kAudioDevicePropertyBufferFrameSize, kAudioUnitScope_Global, 0,
+                                &cur, &cur_size) == noErr){
+            frames = muldiv(stream->period / 2, (int)hw.mSampleRate, 10000000);
+            if(frames > 0 && cur > frames){
+                sc = AudioUnitSetProperty(stream->unit, kAudioDevicePropertyBufferFrameSize, kAudioUnitScope_Global, 0,
+                                          &frames, sizeof(frames));
+                if(sc != noErr)
+                    WARN("Couldn't set the I/O buffer to %u frames: %x\n", (unsigned)frames, (int)sc);
+                else
+                    TRACE("I/O buffer %u -> %u frames at %u Hz\n", (unsigned)cur, (unsigned)frames, (unsigned)hw.mSampleRate);
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
     sc = AudioUnitInitialize(stream->unit);
     if(sc != noErr){
         WARN("Couldn't initialize: %x\n", (int)sc);
